-- #1110 PR C — `medaka lint --fix` over a file dense in INTERFACE-OCCURRENCE
-- carriers (`Constraint` / `Super` / `Require` / `DImpl.iface`).
--
-- WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS.  Five `lint.mdk` rules REBUILD a `DImpl` node in order
-- to rewrite a method body (`lambdaSectionFix`, `ifMaxMinFix`, `andThenPureMapFix`,
-- `exprRuleFix`, `concatToInterpFix` — each matches `d@(DImpl { implOrigin = _,
-- methods })`).  A rebuild through `dImplUnresolved`, or through a total record
-- literal that respells every field, would silently reset an acquired
-- `implOrigin`/`requireOrigin` — and resolve's immunity rule (fill in only a
-- still-unresolved origin) makes such a reset PERMANENT.  Record UPDATE is the
-- discipline that prevents it; this fixture is the corpus that keeps a rule-fixer
-- exercised against the shape.
--
-- 🚨 WHAT IT CAN AND CANNOT PROVE.  `medaka lint` runs on the RAW, PRE-RESOLVE AST,
-- so every origin in this file is `OriginUnresolved` when a fixer sees it: nothing
-- here can OBSERVE an identity being destroyed, and claiming otherwise would be
-- claiming past the evidence.  What the `.fixed` golden does prove is the half that
-- is checkable at this stage and is the one that would destroy source: the fixers
-- still produce the right text over interface-occurrence-dense decls, and the
-- printer does not leak an identity field into the output.  The runtime half — that
-- an acquired origin survives to the trees `elaborateModules` returns — is asserted
-- by test/diff_compiler_origin_agreement.sh's per-carrier `iface-occs-*`
-- anti-vacuity guards, which go to 0 the moment a rebuild blanks a carrier.
--
-- The `.fixed` golden was derived from the rules' own contracts BEFORE it was
-- captured, not read off the tool: rule-if-max-min turns `if p > q then p else q`
-- into `max p q` (and the `<` form into `min`), rule-concat-to-interp turns a
-- two-hole `++` chain into one interpolated literal, and every impl HEAD and
-- `requires` clause must come through byte-identical.

interface Weigh a where
  weigh : a -> Int

interface Label a requires Weigh a where
  label : a -> String

data Crate a = Crate a a

-- `requires` on an impl (a `Require` occurrence) beside a rule-if-max-min body:
-- the fixer must rewrite the METHOD and leave the impl head and its `requires`
-- alone.
impl Weigh (Crate a) requires Weigh a where
  weigh (Crate x y) = max (weigh x) (weigh y)

-- Two `requires` predicates, and a rule-concat-to-interp body (TWO holes — one
-- hole is a documented negative for that rule).
impl Label (Crate a) requires Weigh a, Label a where
  label (Crate x y) = "crate[\{label x},\{label y}]"

-- A `=>` predicate at a top-level signature over a fixable body, so the
-- Constraint carrier is exercised outside an impl too.
describe : Weigh a => a -> a -> Int
describe x y = min (weigh x) (weigh y)

-- NEGATIVE: an interface-dense decl with nothing fixable — the fixer must decline
-- rather than reformat.
plain : Label a => a -> String
plain x = label x
